rsuite CLI reference

bin/rsuite runs YAML scenario files and reports one verdict per
file. Build it with make bin/rsuite. This file documents exact
behavior. Wording in quotes is literal output. Verified by running
the binary with --help and with bad flags. If you pick the wrong
subcommand you will either lint without running or run without
privilege and misread the result.

When to use which subcommand

lint - check file shape without running. Needs privs: no.

run - run files for real. Needs privs: yes, root for netns
plus TUN.

list - see file summary without checks. Needs privs: no.

doctor - check host setup and deps. Needs privs: no.

report - render a run log or run dir. Needs privs: no.

Subcommands

lint with YAML files: Check files without running them. Prints path:
OK per good file on stdout. Prints path plus line errors on stderr.
Needs no privs. Exit 0 when all pass, 1 when any fail.

run with flags plus YAML files: Run files, one child per file. Needs
root for netns plus TUN. Always forks, even at jobs=1, so one code
path serves serial and parallel runs. See flags and log layout
below.

list with YAML files: Parse-only summary per file. Needs no privs.
Parse success does not imply lint success. See shape below.

doctor: Report on setup and deps. Takes no arguments. See probe list
below.

report with a log file or run dir: Render verdict, timeline,
graphs, and checks as text; --html OUT writes the offline HTML
file instead; --format prom prints Prometheus exposition to
stdout (one gauge sample per numeric metric token, key plus
phase plus log labels) for textfile dirs or remote-write.
Malformed lines skip. Needs no privs.

Global and per-command help

Help always prints to stdout and exits 0. Missing or wrong commands
print usage to stderr and exit 2. If you see exit 2, fix the command
line: nothing ran.

rsuite --help and rsuite -h print the global usage plus exit line
plus short paragraphs, exit 0. Each subcommand takes its own help as
the sole arg: lint, list, doctor, and run forms with --help or -h.
Run help works inside the run flag parser in any position before
files. Help output goes to stdout, exit 0. No subcommand or unknown
subcommand prints usage to stderr, exit 2. lint with no file says
lint needs at least one file. list with no file says list needs at
least one file. run with no file says run needs at least one file.
doctor with any arg says doctor takes no arguments. All exit 2.

Run flags

--jobs N and --jobs=N set parallel children. Default 1. Both
spellings work. Missing value says run needs a value after --jobs,
exit 2. Bad text (zero, negative, non-numeric, trailing junk,
leading space) prints rsuite: bad --jobs value plus text plus usage,
exit 2. Over 64 clamps to 64 with a warning on stderr and keeps
going:

  rsuite: --jobs 100 clamped to 64

Verified live with --jobs 100.

--log-dir D and --log-dir=D pick the run log dir. Missing value says
run needs a value after --log-dir, exit 2. Empty value says run
needs a non-empty --log-dir, exit 2. Unusable dir prints rsuite: no
log dir plus dir plus errno and keeps going without file logs. See
log layout.

--tap and --json pick machine output. Default (neither) prints the
same TAP bytes but also draws progress on a tty. The two flags
conflict: with both set the CLI prints rsuite: --tap and --json
conflict plus usage, exit 2. Verified live.

-h and --help inside run print run help, exit 0. Unknown flag prints
rsuite: unknown flag plus flag plus usage, exit 2.

Flags must precede files. The parser stops at the first non-flag.
Any later arg starting with a dash is an error:

  rsuite: flag <flag> after files;
  flags precede files

plus usage, exit 2. Verified live with a trailing --jobs.

Exit codes

Zero means the command worked, one means the suite found failure,
two means the command line was wrong. Skips never fail the run. If
you alert on any nonzero, all-skip runs will page you for healthy
hosts.

0 ok. 1 suite failure (some file failed; skips do not fail the run).
2 bad usage (bad flags, missing files, conflict, unknown
subcommand).

Child skip 2 versus usage 2 is a known collision across domains. The
child exit for skip is 2, the same number as the CLI usage exit.
They never mix: the parent maps child 0 to pass, 2 to skip, and
anything else (plus killed children) to fail, then tallies pass,
fail, and skip. The final CLI exit is 1 when fail count is above 0
else 0. An all-skip run exits 0. Usage errors exit 2 before any
child starts.

TAP and JSON bytes

Machine output is one line per file in input order after all work
finishes, then one summary line. No plan and no version lines. If
you parse it, expect exactly this shape: pars ers that want a plan
line will fail.

TAP per file:

  ok 1 path (pass)
  ok 2 path # SKIP why (skip)
  not ok 3 path - why (fail)

Newlines and returns in why print as spaces. TAP summary:

  # pass=1 fail=0 skip=0

On a color tty progress run the three counts print in bold with
green, red, and dim inks. Piped runs and any --tap or --json run
stay plain. The verified plain run with a missing file prints:

  not ok 1 /nonexistent.yaml -
  No such file or directory
  # pass=0 fail=1 skip=0

exit 1.

JSON per file:

  {"file":"path","verdict":"pass",
  "why":"text"}

Verdict words are pass, fail, and skip. Strings quote with escapes
for quote, backslash, newline, tab, return, and unicode escapes for
other control bytes. JSON summary:

  {"summary":{"pass":0,"fail":1,
  "skip":0}}

JSON never colors. Verified live with --json on a missing file.

Progress rows

Live rows are a human display only. They appear when stdout is a tty
and neither machine flag is set. Piped output never draws rows, so
TAP bytes stay stable for scripts. If rows vanish in your logs, that
is expected: the run still tested everything.

Each live row reads:

  RUNNING  <base>  <phase> [i/n]

Base is the file name after the last slash. Phase is the last suite:
line seen, with the suite: prefix stripped. Index pair is 0-based
phase index over phase total, from suite: phase and suite: frac scan
lines. Unknown phase (count, blank name, unreadable file) prints
phase only with no tail. State word prints yellow on color terms.

At most 8 rows draw. The rest fold into one tail line:

  ... +N more

Long lines cut the middle to terminal width with ... between,
keeping head and tail. Rows repaint in place with cursor-up codes.
Dumb terms (TERM is dumb) or any NO_COLOR value scroll instead with
zero escape codes at all. There is no head line, no counts, no bars,
no percent, no elapsed, and no spinners. After the loop a newline
prints, then result lines, then the summary, then (progress mode
only) a failure list.

Log dir layout

Every run keeps its logs. Nothing cleans them, so budget disk and
remove them by hand when done. If a run loses its log dir, the
verdict still stands: bytes are phase scanned even with a null log,
but post-mortem gets harder.

Default: a fresh per-run dir from mkdtemp under /var/tmp, shaped
as resonance-rsuite-XXXXXX. With --log-dir D: mkdir the dir (exists
stat it back, and reject non-directories instead of degrading.

Per file: base plus numeric suffix log in the run dir, opened
exclusive with a numeric suffix on clash (up to 100 tries). Null log
keeps the run; bytes are still phase scanned. Child stdio is
redirected into a pipe whose bytes the parent logs and scans for
suite: phase and frac lines.

Logs persist per file in the run dir. Nothing cleans them. Missing
or empty logs report (no log). In progress mode only, failed files
list after the summary as:

  # failures:
  # <file> log <path>

Red on a color tty, plain otherwise. Piped TAP stays byte-identical.

Forensics live one layer down in the executor: it tails node logs
(node logs, or weld plus ros logs for weld) to stderr and copies
journals beside the run statedir before the wipe, mirroring check
forensics, then prints suite: forensics at plus dir.

list output shape

List shows what the file declares, not whether it is valid. A clean
list line does not mean lint passes: run lint before you run. If a
key is missing you get a warning on stderr but still a summary line.

One line per file on stdout:

  path: name=X sut=Y nodes=N
  phases=N [a, b]

Missing scalar prints a dash. Missing key warns on stderr but still
prints:

  path: warning: no 'key'; lint fails

Checked keys are name, version, sut, nodes, timeout_s, and phases.
Names with blanks, quotes, or slashes print double quoted. Read or
parse failure prints path plus errno or path plus line plus col plus
message on stderr and marks the run failed. Success does not imply
lint success by design.

doctor probes

Doctor checks whether this host can run scenarios. It informs, never
fails for missing tools: exit stays 0 with the report, and 1 only on
probe error. If a run skips, read these nine lines before anything
else.

Nine lines, one per check, shaped as what: OK, what: MISSING plus
hint, or what: ERROR plus hint. Verified live:

  euid root (MISSING when not root)
  /dev/net/tun (needs read plus write)
  netns list; create needs root
  (probes ip netns list)
  bin-dir/concord (needs exec)
  bin-dir/weld (needs exec)
  docker daemon (probes docker info)
  run lock (needs read plus write)
  run counter (needs read plus write)
  lock dir /var/tmp (needs write plus
  exec)

Sibling paths resolve from the exe path plus leaf, with a ./bin
fallback. Exit 0 with the report even when lines say MISSING. Exit 1
only on probe error (fork or wait failure). Creating netns and TUN
ids needs root; missing deps inform, they do not fail.

Ctrl-C behavior

Interrupt stops launch at once and kills live children with a grace
period, then sweeps orphans so no netns or TUN leaks. Files never
launched count as fail, not skip. If you press Ctrl-C twice, the
second still lands: the loop kills on its tick and children restore
default handling so they die directly.

SIGINT and SIGTERM set a flag; the run loop does the killing on the
next 100 ms tick. Launch stops at once. Live children get TERM
first, then KILL past a 2 s grace. Each child owns its process group
(setpgid in child and parent, with pid fallback when the group is
gone), so grandchildren die too and no orphans keep netns or TUN.
After reap, each group gets an orphan sweep (TERM, wait 2 s in 100
ms steps, then KILL). Files never launched are marked interrupted
and count as fail. Forked children restore default signal handling
so Ctrl-C still kills them directly.

Code map

Subcommand dispatch with help and arity errors; jobs and log dir
parsing with clamp and conflict rules; usage and exit-code shared
paths; TAP and JSON line plus summary printers with tally mapping;
progress row cap, fold, trim, color, and dumb handling; log dir
create, join, open with clash tries, and persist rule with failure
list; list summary shape with warning and quoting rules; doctor
ordered probes with sibling resolve and probe helper; interrupt
flag, grace kill, group, and sweep path.
